# Slice shape — telling a vertical slice from a horizontal layer
## What this file is for
`tdd` says how to **cut** a slice. This file says how to **recognise** that the
work already underway was cut the wrong way. The two are different jobs and they
happen at different moments: cutting happens before anything is written, and
recognising happens when three files are open, the branch is half done, and the
question is whether to keep going.
Phase 3 of `plan` reads this file. So does the mid-course re-entry — the moment
you have been deep in an implementation and no longer know whether it still fits
the whole.
## The two shapes
| | Horizontal layer | Vertical slice |
|---|---|---|
| What one step finishes | **a level** — every model, every handler | **a behaviour** — one path end to end |
| What runs at the end of step one | nothing a user can reach | one thing a user can reach |
| When the design error shows up | at integration, after all the steps | inside the step, immediately |
| What a failed step costs | the layers below it are already built on it | one slice |
| What it looks like on a plan | orderly, symmetric, easy to estimate | uneven, lumpy, hard to estimate |
**A horizontal layer finishes a level; a vertical slice finishes a behaviour.**
That is the whole distinction, and every test below is a way of asking it about
a plan that does not announce which one it is.
## The antipattern in the form it actually appears
The horizontal plan is not caught by looking at it, because it looks like order.
```
[X] Phase 1: define every model
Phase 2: implement all service logic
Phase 3: implement every API endpoint
```
```
[O] Slice 1: retrieve an empty list — happy path
Slice 2: create one item (e2e)
Slice 3: update an item
Slice 4: error handling and edge cases
```
The left one is easier to defend in a review: the steps are the same size, the
dependencies point one way, nobody has to argue about which behaviour comes
first. That is exactly why it survives. **Ask what runs at the end of step one.**
If the answer is "nothing yet, but the next step needs it", the plan is
horizontal no matter how it is labelled.
## Four judgments
Run all four. Each one catches a different drift, and three of them pass
comfortably on work that the fourth condemns.
| Axis | Ask | The drift it catches |
|---|---|---|
| **Pattern consistency** | Does something similar already exist here, and does this follow it? | A second way of doing what the repository already does one way |
| **Complexity** | Is this more machinery than the problem has? | Abstraction built for a case that has not arrived |
| **Boundaries** | Is this code in the right layer, and is the responsibility split where the layer says? | Logic that migrated into whichever file was open |
| **Missing patterns** | Which existing patterns — error handling, logging, types — is this not using? | Reinvention that reads as new work |
Complexity and boundaries pull against each other on purpose. Cutting machinery
tends to collapse layers, and respecting layers tends to add machinery; if both
axes are comfortable at once, at least one of them was not actually asked.
## Reporting
Three categories, in this order. The order matters: a report that opens with
problems gets read as a verdict on the author, and a re-entry check that reads
as a verdict does not get run again.
```
[OK] What works well:
- ...
[WARN] What needs improvement:
- [problem] -> [proposal]
[REVIEW] What needs reconsideration:
- [current approach] vs [alternative]
Reason: ...
```
`[WARN]` items carry a proposal or they are not findings. `[REVIEW]` items carry
a named alternative and a reason, because "this feels wrong" is not something
the next person can act on.
## Four actions
One of these, chosen explicitly, ends the check.
| Action | When |
|---|---|
| **Continue** | The direction holds. Say so — a check that only ever finds problems stops being run |
| **Refactor, then continue** | The shape is right and one part drifted |
| **Recut the slices** | Step one finishes a level rather than a behaviour |
| **Back to Phase 1** | The whole approach conflicts with a pattern the repository already committed to |
Recutting is cheaper than it looks, and it gets more expensive every slice. The
horizontal plan's cost is paid at integration, which is after the point where
recutting is still an option.
## What this file does not settle
- **Whether the requirement is right.** This is a shape check, not a review of
what was asked for. A perfectly vertical slice of the wrong feature passes.
- **How small a slice should be.** Sizing is judgment; the only rule here is
that each finished slice adds one thing that runs.
- **Whether an abstraction is premature.** It forces the question and records
the answer. The answer is the author's, and it belongs in the spec.